"""
# Cross-module tuple positional field read Specification

**Feature IDs:** N/A
**Category:** Runtime
**Difficulty:** 3/5
**Status:** Fixed
**Requirements:** N/A
**Plan:** N/A
**Design:** N/A
**Research:** doc/08_tracking/bug/jit_cross_module_tuple_field_read_returns_nil_2026-08-01.md

## Overview

`jit_cross_module_tuple_field_read_returns_nil_2026-08-01` reported that a
tuple built by a function in ANOTHER module read back `nil` through positional
field access (`yaml_string("hi").0` -> `nil`, expected `"string"`), while a
predicate compiled **alongside** the constructor (`is_yaml_scalar`) read the
same tuple correctly. The value was intact; only the cross-module positional
read was wrong, so a caller branching on `v.0 == "string"` took the false
branch forever with no diagnostic.

The record left three things explicitly "not yet established": whether the
index matters (`.0` vs `.1`), whether it is tuples specifically, and whether
the native path shares it. This spec settles the first two on the current seed
by asserting both positions and by pairing each cross-module read with the
same-module predicate that was always correct — that pairing is the whole
point, because a same-module accessor stayed green throughout the defect and a
guard built only from predicates would be vacuous.

Re-checked 2026-09-12: both positions read correctly on both lanes.

## Syntax

```simple
use std.common.yaml.types.{yaml_string}
yaml_string("hi").0        # must be "string", not nil
```

"""

use std.spec
use std.common.yaml.types.{yaml_string, yaml_number, yaml_boolean, is_yaml_scalar, is_yaml_string, is_yaml_number}


# ============================================================================
# Test Group 1: the record's own reproducer
# ============================================================================

describe "positional read of a tuple built in another module":
    """
    ## `yaml_string("hi")`

    Position 0 is the tag the defect returned `nil` for; position 1 is the
    payload the record never measured.
    """

    it "reads position 0 as the tag, not nil":
        val v = yaml_string("hi")
        expect(v.0).to_equal("string")

    it "reads position 1 as the payload":
        val v = yaml_string("hi")
        expect(v.1).to_equal("hi")

    it "reads position 0 of a differently-tagged constructor":
        val v = yaml_boolean(true)
        expect(v.0).to_equal("boolean")


# ============================================================================
# Test Group 2: the same-module predicate control
# ============================================================================

describe "same-module predicate over the same tuple":
    """
    ## Control

    These were correct while the defect was live. They are kept so a future
    failure can be attributed to the cross-module READ rather than to the
    constructor producing a wrong tuple.
    """

    it "classifies a string node as a scalar":
        expect(is_yaml_scalar(yaml_string("hi"))).to_equal(true)

    it "classifies a string node as a string":
        expect(is_yaml_string(yaml_string("hi"))).to_equal(true)


# ============================================================================
# Test Group 3: the caller-branch shape that stayed silently wrong
# ============================================================================

describe "branching on a cross-module positional read":
    """
    ## The silent-wrong-result shape

    `v.0 == "string"` is the comparison that took the false branch forever.
    Both polarities are pinned.
    """

    it "takes the true branch for a matching tag":
        val v = yaml_string("hi")
        expect(v.0 == "string").to_equal(true)

    it "takes the false branch for a non-matching tag":
        val v = yaml_number(1.0)
        expect(v.0 == "string").to_equal(false)

    it "agrees with the same-module predicate on a number node":
        val v = yaml_number(1.0)
        expect(is_yaml_number(v)).to_equal(true)
